<code>assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) == null) == (this.get(i) == null) && (\old(this.get(i)) != null) ==> this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) ==> \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) == null) ==> (this.get(i) == null) && (\old(this.get(i)) != null) ==> (this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) ==> \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) == null) == (\result == null || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>

However, the above assertion can be simplified to focus on the elements' values directly without comparing null explicitly:

<code>assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) ==> \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>

This captures the essence that for every valid index in the old list, the element at that index remains unchanged after the removal of the last element.
